# Commit as you go

**Do not wait to be asked to commit.** When a piece of work is finished and
verified, commit it. Leaving a session's work uncommitted is how the repo and
the deployed app drift apart, and it has already happened once: production was
deployed straight from a dirty working tree.

## What counts as finished

A change is ready to commit when it builds, its verification scripts pass, and
it would still make sense to someone reading it a month from now. Do not wait
for the whole task to be done — commit each coherent piece as it lands.

## One commit per coherent change

Split unrelated work. Fixing a keeper declaration and repairing a draft test
are two commits, not one. Do not split so far that a commit does not build.

## Message style

Match the existing log. Imperative subject line that names the effect, not the
mechanism, then a body explaining **why** — the failure it prevents, the thing
that was wrong, the reason a reader would otherwise be surprised.

```
Stop the two-client draft test passing by never running

The one test that proves a remote manager sees a pick had not asserted
anything in some time. It waited on a `<footer>` that no longer exists...

Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>
```

Always include the `Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>` trailer.

## Still ask first

- Pushing to a remote, force-pushing, rebasing, or amending a pushed commit.
- Anything that looks like a credential: `.env.local`, keys, tokens.
